[]($section)
## General Technical Recommendations
These are things that I think provide fundamentally useful information for building good
software. It is extremely worthwhile to read/watch them all (unless otherwise noted), when
you have time.

* [The Grug Brained Developer](https://grugbrain.dev/) is wall to wall a very good set of
thoughts. If English is your second language, I apologize.
* [Google's Code Review Guidelines](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/). Read
both sections.
* [Write code top-down](https://www.teamten.com/lawrence/programming/write-code-top-down.html) will
save you a lot of pain, but takes some practice. I am still shit at it.
* Watch [this talk from Scott Meyers](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WDIkqP4JbkE) on CPU
caches. Regardless of how high level you work day to day, eventually your code runs on a
CPU.
    * [This talk from Mike Acton](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rX0ItVEVjHc) is in a
    similar vein and a good follow up.
    * As is [What Every Programmer Should Know About Memory](https://people.freebsd.org/~lstewart/articles/cpumemory.pdf),
    though be warned that this is very long, very technical, and very dry. Useful though!
* [Designing Data-Intensive Applications](https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/designing-data-intensive-applications/9781491903063/)
is a phenomenal textbook on all things database and database adjacent. Available from most
online book sellers, or if you work with me in person I'm happy to lend you my copy.

[]($section)
## Personal Recommendations
These are things that heavily influence how ***I personally*** work. I will likely reference
them often, but you should form your own opinions on them.

* [Why some of us like "interdiff" code review](https://gist.github.com/thoughtpolice/9c45287550a56b2047c6311fbadebed2) and [How Jane Street Does Code Review](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MUqvXHEjmus)
are the reasons I evangalize [`jj`](https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj) so much. Also, you
should try `jj` if I haven't already said that to you a thousand times. [Here's the link,
again](https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj). Try `jj`.
* [Locality of Behavior](https://htmx.org/essays/locality-of-behaviour/) (Written by the
same guy who wrote grugbrain!) is a good juxtaposition to DRY/SoC/etc.
* [How I Build Software Quickly](https://evanhahn.com/how-i-build-software-quickly/)
from Evan Hahn is just a very solid set of ideas, even if many of them are pretty
non-revolutionary. Sometimes you just gotta see them written down.
* You probably already know that I don't generally like LLM coding tools. You may use one,
and that's fine and your perogative. I would encourage you to read [Language Models as Thespians](https://jstrieb.github.io/posts/llm-thespians/),
just as a way to frame what the tool is doing (it's not a rant railing against AI, I
promise).
* If you're a book person, you can get [tons of free, open source programming books](https://github.com/EbookFoundation/free-programming-books)
